1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,287
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is AP Computer Science A,

2
00:00:04,287 --> 00:00:08,519
Unit 2, part two: topics two point seven
to two point twelve. We'll make our

3
00:00:08,519 --> 00:00:12,974
programs repeat work with while loops and
for loops, trace a full worked example

4
00:00:12,974 --> 00:00:17,428
after every topic, and finish with one
complete Java program that uses the whole

5
00:00:17,428 --> 00:00:17,707
unit.

6
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:23,733
Printing the numbers one to five takes
five print lines. One to a thousand would

7
00:00:23,733 --> 00:00:24,704
take a thousand.

8
00:00:25,154 --> 00:00:26,455
A loop does it in three.

9
00:00:27,605 --> 00:00:31,339
A while loop repeats its body as long as
its condition is true.

10
00:00:31,789 --> 00:00:36,709
Java checks the condition first. If it's
true, the body runs, then Java checks

11
00:00:36,709 --> 00:00:39,106
again. When it's false, the loop ends.

12
00:00:40,256 --> 00:00:42,069
Here's a loop that prints one to five.

13
00:00:42,519 --> 00:00:48,258
int i equals one. While i is less than or
equal to five, print i, then add one to i.

14
00:00:48,708 --> 00:00:53,145
Every working loop has three parts: start
a variable, test it, and change it.

15
00:00:54,295 --> 00:00:58,455
Let's trace it. i is one, the test is
true, print one, i becomes two.

16
00:00:58,905 --> 00:01:04,387
This repeats until i is six. Six less than
or equal to five is false, so the loop

17
00:01:04,387 --> 00:01:04,793
stops.

18
00:01:05,243 --> 00:01:06,801
The body ran five times.

19
00:01:07,951 --> 00:01:11,812
If the condition is false at the start,
the body never runs at all.

20
00:01:12,262 --> 00:01:16,735
If nothing in the body ever makes the
condition false, the loop runs forever.

21
00:01:16,735 --> 00:01:18,129
That's an infinite loop.

22
00:01:18,579 --> 00:01:21,117
Forgetting i plus plus is the classic
cause.

23
00:01:22,267 --> 00:01:26,982
An off by one error is when a loop runs
one time too many, or one too few.

24
00:01:27,432 --> 00:01:32,805
Less than versus less than or equal to is
usually the culprit. Always check the

25
00:01:32,805 --> 00:01:34,301
first and last values.

26
00:01:35,451 --> 00:01:40,896
Your turn. int n equals ten. While n is
greater than zero, n minus equals three.

27
00:01:40,896 --> 00:01:42,598
What is n after the loop?

28
00:01:43,048 --> 00:01:44,243
Pause the video now.

29
00:01:44,693 --> 00:01:50,210
n goes ten, seven, four, one, then
negative two. Negative two isn't greater

30
00:01:50,210 --> 00:01:52,565
than zero, so n is negative two.

31
00:01:53,015 --> 00:01:56,001
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

32
00:01:57,151 --> 00:02:01,226
Now let's work through a complete while
loop example, one step at a time.

33
00:02:02,376 --> 00:02:09,341
The problem: add up the digits of a whole
number. For 4072, the answer is 4 plus 0

34
00:02:09,341 --> 00:02:11,634
plus 7 plus 2, which is 13.

35
00:02:12,084 --> 00:02:16,479
We don't know in advance how many digits
there are, so a while loop is a good fit.

36
00:02:17,629 --> 00:02:19,528
Here's the code. Start sum at zero.

37
00:02:19,978 --> 00:02:25,140
While n is greater than zero, n mod ten
gives the last digit. Add it to sum.

38
00:02:25,590 --> 00:02:30,582
Then n divided by ten, using integer
division, chops off that last digit.

39
00:02:31,032 --> 00:02:33,976
When n reaches zero, every digit has been
added.

40
00:02:35,126 --> 00:02:40,801
Let's trace it with a table. At the start,
n is 4072 and sum is zero.

41
00:02:41,251 --> 00:02:46,734
Pass one: the last digit is 2. sum becomes
2, and n becomes 407.

42
00:02:47,184 --> 00:02:50,320
Pass two: digit 7. sum is 9, n is 40.

43
00:02:50,770 --> 00:02:54,247
Pass three: digit 0. sum stays 9, n is 4.

44
00:02:54,697 --> 00:03:00,382
Pass four: digit 4. sum is 13, and n
becomes zero. The condition is now false,

45
00:03:00,382 --> 00:03:01,694
so the loop stops.

46
00:03:02,844 --> 00:03:06,962
The result is 13, after exactly four
passes, one per digit.

47
00:03:07,412 --> 00:03:12,321
Notice the three parts of every while
loop: n starts at the number, the test is

48
00:03:12,321 --> 00:03:17,292
n greater than zero, and n slash equals
ten moves it toward the end. Forget that

49
00:03:17,292 --> 00:03:19,529
last line, and the loop never stops.

50
00:03:20,679 --> 00:03:26,451
Your turn. Run the same code with n equal
to 500. How many times does the loop body

51
00:03:26,451 --> 00:03:27,911
run, and what is sum?

52
00:03:28,361 --> 00:03:29,556
Pause the video now.

53
00:03:30,006 --> 00:03:37,472
Three times: n is 500, then 50, then 5.
The digits are 0, 0, and 5, so sum is 5.

54
00:03:37,922 --> 00:03:39,629
Next, 2.8, for loops.

55
00:03:40,779 --> 00:03:43,830
Most loops count: start here, stop there,
step by one.

56
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:47,394
Java has a loop built exactly for that:
the for loop.

57
00:03:48,544 --> 00:03:51,424
A for loop puts all three loop parts on
one line.

58
00:03:51,874 --> 00:03:58,100
for, open parenthesis, int i equals zero,
semicolon, i less than five, semicolon, i

59
00:03:58,100 --> 00:03:58,850
plus plus.

60
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:00,858
Initialize, test, update.

61
00:04:02,008 --> 00:04:03,693
Here's the order Java follows.

62
00:04:04,143 --> 00:04:09,178
Initialize once. Then test. If true, run
the body, then update, then test again.

63
00:04:09,628 --> 00:04:12,038
The update always happens after the body.

64
00:04:13,188 --> 00:04:16,858
Any for loop can be written as a while
loop, and the other way around.

65
00:04:17,308 --> 00:04:21,964
Use for when you know how many times to
repeat. Use while when you repeat until

66
00:04:21,964 --> 00:04:23,025
something happens.

67
00:04:24,175 --> 00:04:28,143
Counting how many times a loop runs is a
favorite AP question.

68
00:04:28,593 --> 00:04:32,796
For i from zero while i is less than n,
the body runs n times.

69
00:04:33,246 --> 00:04:38,344
For i from one while i is less than or
equal to n, it also runs n times.

70
00:04:38,794 --> 00:04:42,528
And with i plus equals two, it runs about
half as many times.

71
00:04:43,678 --> 00:04:46,622
The variable i only exists inside the for
loop.

72
00:04:47,072 --> 00:04:50,506
Try to use it after the loop ends, and
you'll get a compile error.

73
00:04:51,656 --> 00:04:57,222
Your turn. How many times does this print?
for int k equals three, k less than ten, k

74
00:04:57,222 --> 00:04:58,270
plus equals two.

75
00:04:58,720 --> 00:04:59,914
Pause the video now.

76
00:05:00,364 --> 00:05:04,482
k takes the values three, five, seven, and
nine. That's four times.

77
00:05:04,932 --> 00:05:07,918
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

78
00:05:09,069 --> 00:05:12,866
Let's trace a for loop carefully, the way
the AP exam expects.

79
00:05:14,016 --> 00:05:20,135
The problem: add up the odd numbers from
one to nine. That's 1 plus 3 plus 5 plus 7

80
00:05:20,135 --> 00:05:20,651
plus 9.

81
00:05:21,801 --> 00:05:23,998
Here's the code. total starts at zero.

82
00:05:24,448 --> 00:05:29,690
The for loop starts i at one, keeps going
while i is less than ten, and adds two to

83
00:05:29,690 --> 00:05:30,827
i after each pass.

84
00:05:31,277 --> 00:05:32,898
Inside, we add i to total.

85
00:05:34,048 --> 00:05:36,352
Trace it. i is 1, total becomes 1.

86
00:05:36,802 --> 00:05:38,360
i is 3, total is 4.

87
00:05:38,810 --> 00:05:40,452
i is 5, total is 9.

88
00:05:40,902 --> 00:05:42,694
i is 7, total is 16.

89
00:05:43,144 --> 00:05:45,192
i is 9, total is 25.

90
00:05:45,642 --> 00:05:50,144
Then i becomes 11. 11 is not less than 10,
so the loop ends.

91
00:05:51,294 --> 00:05:54,280
The loop ran five times, and total is 25.

92
00:05:54,730 --> 00:06:00,994
To count passes in a for loop, list the
values the variable takes. Here: 1, 3, 5,

93
00:06:00,994 --> 00:06:03,392
7, 9. Five values, five passes.

94
00:06:03,842 --> 00:06:06,722
And notice: after the loop, i is 11, not
9.

95
00:06:07,872 --> 00:06:12,902
Your turn. What does this loop print? i
starts at ten, the loop runs while i is

96
00:06:12,902 --> 00:06:16,341
greater than zero, and i goes down by
three each time.

97
00:06:16,791 --> 00:06:17,986
Pause the video now.

98
00:06:18,436 --> 00:06:22,852
10, 7, 4, 1. The next value, negative 2,
fails the test.

99
00:06:23,302 --> 00:06:25,670
Next, 2.9, standard algorithms.

100
00:06:26,819 --> 00:06:31,232
Loops and if statements are building
blocks. Put them together, and you can

101
00:06:31,232 --> 00:06:32,408
solve real problems.

102
00:06:32,858 --> 00:06:37,061
The AP exam expects you to know a handful
of standard algorithms.

103
00:06:38,211 --> 00:06:41,176
The first is the accumulator. It builds up
a total.

104
00:06:41,626 --> 00:06:45,424
Start sum at zero. Loop through the
values, adding each one to sum.

105
00:06:45,874 --> 00:06:48,519
Divide sum by the count, and you have the
average.

106
00:06:49,669 --> 00:06:54,316
The second is counting. Start count at
zero. Inside the loop, an if decides

107
00:06:54,316 --> 00:06:55,493
whether to add one.

108
00:06:55,943 --> 00:07:00,530
For example, count how many numbers from
one to twenty are multiples of three.

109
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:04,026
The third is finding a minimum or maximum.

110
00:07:04,476 --> 00:07:09,405
Start with the first value as your best so
far. For each value after that, if it

111
00:07:09,405 --> 00:07:11,068
beats the best, replace it.

112
00:07:12,218 --> 00:07:15,525
The fourth checks a property of a number,
digit by digit.

113
00:07:15,975 --> 00:07:20,455
n percent ten gives the last digit. n
divided by ten removes it.

114
00:07:20,905 --> 00:07:25,513
Loop while n is greater than zero, and you
can add up the digits of any number.

115
00:07:26,663 --> 00:07:31,292
The fifth asks whether at least one value,
or every value, meets a condition.

116
00:07:31,742 --> 00:07:35,774
For at least one, start with false, and
set it to true when you find a match.

117
00:07:36,224 --> 00:07:41,024
For every value, start with true, and set
it to false when you find one that fails.

118
00:07:42,174 --> 00:07:46,695
Your turn. Using the digit trick, what is
the sum of the digits of four hundred

119
00:07:46,695 --> 00:07:47,038
seven?

120
00:07:47,488 --> 00:07:48,683
Pause the video now.

121
00:07:49,133 --> 00:07:52,077
Seven, then zero, then four. The sum is
eleven.

122
00:07:52,527 --> 00:07:55,514
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

123
00:07:56,664 --> 00:08:00,888
Here's a standard algorithm you can build
with one loop and one condition.

124
00:08:02,038 --> 00:08:08,599
The problem: find the smallest factor of a
number greater than one. For 91, that's 7,

125
00:08:08,599 --> 00:08:10,529
because 7 times 13 is 91.

126
00:08:10,979 --> 00:08:14,541
If the smallest factor is the number
itself, the number is prime.

127
00:08:15,691 --> 00:08:17,483
Here's the code. Start f at two.

128
00:08:17,933 --> 00:08:22,712
While num mod f is not zero, f doesn't
divide num, so add one to f.

129
00:08:23,162 --> 00:08:25,829
When the loop stops, f is the smallest
factor.

130
00:08:26,979 --> 00:08:31,736
Trace it for 91. f is 2: 91 mod 2 is 1,
keep going.

131
00:08:32,186 --> 00:08:35,429
f is 3: remainder 1. f is 4: remainder 3.

132
00:08:35,879 --> 00:08:39,143
f is 5: remainder 1. f is 6: remainder 1.

133
00:08:39,593 --> 00:08:45,310
f is 7: remainder 0. The test is false,
and the loop stops with f equal to 7.

134
00:08:46,460 --> 00:08:50,791
The smallest factor of 91 is 7, so 91 is
not prime.

135
00:08:51,241 --> 00:08:55,955
This loop always stops, because when f
reaches num, num mod num is zero.

136
00:08:56,405 --> 00:08:59,819
To check for a prime, test whether f
equals num at the end.

137
00:09:00,969 --> 00:09:05,001
Your turn. What is the smallest factor of
77? And of 13?

138
00:09:05,451 --> 00:09:06,645
Pause the video now.

139
00:09:07,095 --> 00:09:14,882
77 gives 7, because 77 is 7 times 11. 13
gives 13, so 13 is prime.

140
00:09:15,332 --> 00:09:17,956
Next, 2.10, String algorithms.

141
00:09:19,106 --> 00:09:23,778
A String is a sequence of characters, so
loops and Strings go together naturally.

142
00:09:24,228 --> 00:09:27,791
Let's look at the String algorithms you'll
see on the AP exam.

143
00:09:28,941 --> 00:09:33,954
To visit every character, loop i from zero
while i is less than the string's length.

144
00:09:34,404 --> 00:09:39,951
Use substring of i comma i plus one to get
the character at index i, as a String.

145
00:09:41,101 --> 00:09:43,234
Counting is the same pattern as before.

146
00:09:43,684 --> 00:09:49,227
To count the vowels in a word, loop over
each letter, and add one when the letter

147
00:09:49,227 --> 00:09:50,596
is a, e, i, o, or u.

148
00:09:51,046 --> 00:09:56,685
A neat trick: aeiou dot index of letter is
not negative one, exactly when letter is a

149
00:09:56,685 --> 00:09:57,083
vowel.

150
00:09:58,233 --> 00:10:00,751
To reverse a String, start with an empty
String.

151
00:10:01,201 --> 00:10:05,403
Loop through the characters, and put each
one in front of what you've built so far.

152
00:10:05,853 --> 00:10:08,157
Cat becomes c, then a c, then t a c.

153
00:10:09,307 --> 00:10:13,659
To find a substring, loop over each
starting position, and compare a piece of

154
00:10:13,659 --> 00:10:14,619
the right length.

155
00:10:15,069 --> 00:10:20,181
Be careful with the end. If the piece has
length k, the last start index is length

156
00:10:20,181 --> 00:10:20,680
minus k.

157
00:10:21,830 --> 00:10:24,881
The most common String bug is going one
past the end.

158
00:10:25,331 --> 00:10:30,991
The last valid index is length minus one.
Going further throws a String Index Out Of

159
00:10:30,991 --> 00:10:32,136
Bounds Exception.

160
00:10:33,286 --> 00:10:37,916
Your turn. What does this return for the
word banana? It counts how many times the

161
00:10:37,916 --> 00:10:38,875
letter a appears.

162
00:10:39,325 --> 00:10:40,520
Pause the video now.

163
00:10:40,970 --> 00:10:43,402
banana has three a's, so it returns three.

164
00:10:43,852 --> 00:10:46,839
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

165
00:10:47,989 --> 00:10:53,458
Let's put loops and Strings together in
one full, AP-style worked example, step by

166
00:10:53,458 --> 00:10:53,792
step.

167
00:10:54,942 --> 00:10:59,555
The problem: write a method
countOccurrences that takes two Strings, s

168
00:10:59,555 --> 00:11:03,048
and t, and returns how many times t
appears inside s.

169
00:11:03,498 --> 00:11:06,101
For example, an appears in banana two
times.

170
00:11:07,251 --> 00:11:11,838
Here's the plan. t can start at any index
i where it still fits inside s.

171
00:11:12,288 --> 00:11:16,384
So i goes from zero up to s dot length
minus t dot length.

172
00:11:16,834 --> 00:11:21,314
At each i, take a piece of s the same
length as t, and compare it to t.

173
00:11:22,464 --> 00:11:28,427
Here's the code. Start count at zero. Loop
i from zero while i is less than or equal

174
00:11:28,427 --> 00:11:30,912
to s dot length minus t dot length.

175
00:11:31,362 --> 00:11:37,526
Take s dot substring of i comma i plus t
dot length. If that piece equals t, add

176
00:11:37,526 --> 00:11:40,300
one to count. Finally, return count.

177
00:11:41,450 --> 00:11:47,299
Now let's trace it for banana and an. s
has length six and t has length two, so i

178
00:11:47,299 --> 00:11:48,960
runs from zero to four.

179
00:11:49,410 --> 00:11:52,354
At i equals zero, the piece is b a. Not a
match.

180
00:11:52,804 --> 00:11:55,406
At i equals one, a n. Match. count is one.

181
00:11:55,856 --> 00:11:57,542
At i equals two, n a. No.

182
00:11:57,992 --> 00:12:00,722
At i equals three, a n again. count is
two.

183
00:12:01,172 --> 00:12:05,652
At i equals four, n a. No. The loop ends,
and the method returns two.

184
00:12:06,802 --> 00:12:11,885
Two mistakes to avoid. First, compare
Strings with dot equals, never with double

185
00:12:11,885 --> 00:12:12,648
equals sign.

186
00:12:13,098 --> 00:12:18,664
Second, watch the loop bound. If i runs
all the way to s dot length minus one, the

187
00:12:18,664 --> 00:12:23,619
substring goes past the end, and Java
throws a String Index Out Of Bounds

188
00:12:23,619 --> 00:12:24,298
Exception.

189
00:12:25,448 --> 00:12:30,803
Your turn. What does countOccurrences
return for s equal to a a a a, and t equal

190
00:12:30,803 --> 00:12:31,272
to a a?

191
00:12:31,722 --> 00:12:32,916
Pause the video now.

192
00:12:33,366 --> 00:12:37,748
Three. The matches start at index zero,
one, and two, and they're allowed to

193
00:12:37,748 --> 00:12:38,209
overlap.

194
00:12:38,659 --> 00:12:40,131
Next, nested iteration.

195
00:12:41,281 --> 00:12:45,761
Some problems have two dimensions: rows
and columns, hours and minutes.

196
00:12:46,211 --> 00:12:50,094
For those, we put one loop inside another.
That's nested iteration.

197
00:12:51,244 --> 00:12:54,614
For each pass of the outer loop, the inner
loop runs completely.

198
00:12:55,064 --> 00:12:59,949
If the outer loop runs three times, and
the inner loop runs four times, the body

199
00:12:59,949 --> 00:13:02,147
runs three times four, twelve times.

200
00:13:03,297 --> 00:13:07,137
Here's a loop that prints a rectangle of
stars, three rows of four.

201
00:13:07,587 --> 00:13:12,222
The outer loop picks the row. The inner
loop prints the stars in that row. Then

202
00:13:12,222 --> 00:13:14,158
print line moves to the next row.

203
00:13:15,308 --> 00:13:17,505
The inner loop can depend on the outer
loop.

204
00:13:17,955 --> 00:13:23,150
If the inner loop runs from zero up to the
row number, each row gets one more star,

205
00:13:23,150 --> 00:13:24,590
and you get a triangle.

206
00:13:25,040 --> 00:13:29,157
Now the total is one plus two plus three,
not a simple product.

207
00:13:30,307 --> 00:13:32,526
Nested loops also compare every pair.

208
00:13:32,976 --> 00:13:37,322
To check whether a String has a repeated
letter, compare each letter with every

209
00:13:37,322 --> 00:13:38,202
letter after it.

210
00:13:39,352 --> 00:13:41,592
When tracing nested loops, make a table.

211
00:13:42,042 --> 00:13:46,661
Write the outer variable, then the inner
variable for each pass. It keeps you from

212
00:13:46,661 --> 00:13:47,674
losing your place.

213
00:13:48,824 --> 00:13:54,669
Your turn. The outer loop runs i from zero
to two. The inner loop runs j from zero to

214
00:13:54,669 --> 00:13:57,144
i. How many times does the body run?

215
00:13:57,594 --> 00:13:58,789
Pause the video now.

216
00:13:59,239 --> 00:14:04,332
When i is zero, once. When i is one,
twice. When i is two, three times. That's

217
00:14:04,332 --> 00:14:04,594
six.

218
00:14:05,044 --> 00:14:08,030
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

219
00:14:09,180 --> 00:14:12,764
Nested loops are easy to get wrong. Let's
trace one slowly.

220
00:14:13,914 --> 00:14:18,925
The problem: what does this code print?
The outer loop runs r from one to three.

221
00:14:18,925 --> 00:14:22,746
The inner loop runs c from one up to r,
and prints r times c.

222
00:14:23,896 --> 00:14:26,371
Here's the code. The outer loop picks the
row, r.

223
00:14:26,821 --> 00:14:31,023
The inner loop runs c from one to r, so
row r has r numbers.

224
00:14:31,473 --> 00:14:34,439
After the inner loop ends, println moves
to a new line.

225
00:14:35,589 --> 00:14:39,599
Trace it with a table. r is 1: c is 1,
print 1. New line.

226
00:14:40,049 --> 00:14:43,953
r is 2: c is 1, print 2. c is 2, print 4.
New line.

227
00:14:44,403 --> 00:14:50,355
r is 3: c is 1, print 3. c is 2, print 6.
c is 3, print 9. New line.

228
00:14:50,805 --> 00:14:53,216
Then r becomes 4, and the outer loop ends.

229
00:14:54,366 --> 00:14:58,590
The output is three lines: 1. Then 2, 4.
Then 3, 6, 9.

230
00:14:59,040 --> 00:15:02,859
The inner body ran 1 plus 2 plus 3, which
is 6 times.

231
00:15:03,309 --> 00:15:07,998
Tip: when the inner loop depends on the
outer variable, count the passes row by

232
00:15:07,998 --> 00:15:09,303
row, then add them up.

233
00:15:10,453 --> 00:15:15,309
Your turn. Change the outer loop to run r
from one to four. How many numbers are

234
00:15:15,309 --> 00:15:16,341
printed in total?

235
00:15:16,791 --> 00:15:17,986
Pause the video now.

236
00:15:18,436 --> 00:15:21,721
1 plus 2 plus 3 plus 4, which is 10
numbers.

237
00:15:22,171 --> 00:15:25,585
Next, 2.12, informal run-time analysis.

238
00:15:26,735 --> 00:15:30,746
Two programs can give the same answer, but
one can be much faster.

239
00:15:31,196 --> 00:15:35,292
Run time analysis is a way to compare how
much work code does.

240
00:15:36,442 --> 00:15:40,218
On the AP exam, this means counting
statement executions.

241
00:15:40,668 --> 00:15:44,038
How many times does a line run, given the
size of the input?

242
00:15:45,188 --> 00:15:48,602
A single loop from zero to n runs its body
n times.

243
00:15:49,052 --> 00:15:50,673
Double n, and the work doubles.

244
00:15:51,823 --> 00:15:56,848
Two nested loops, each running n times,
run the inner body n times n, or n

245
00:15:56,848 --> 00:15:57,391
squared.

246
00:15:57,841 --> 00:16:00,806
Double n, and the work becomes four times
as much.

247
00:16:01,956 --> 00:16:04,282
Loops that change their step change the
count.

248
00:16:04,732 --> 00:16:09,724
A loop where i doubles each time, one,
two, four, eight, reaches n in very few

249
00:16:09,724 --> 00:16:10,108
steps.

250
00:16:10,558 --> 00:16:13,992
For n equal to a thousand, that's only
about ten passes.

251
00:16:15,142 --> 00:16:20,265
Here's a strategy. Find the innermost
statement. Count how many times each loop

252
00:16:20,265 --> 00:16:24,934
runs. Multiply nested counts, and add
loops that come one after another.

253
00:16:26,084 --> 00:16:31,714
Your turn. A loop runs i from zero to n.
Right after it, a separate loop runs j

254
00:16:31,714 --> 00:16:36,132
from zero to n. How many times do the two
bodies run in total?

255
00:16:36,582 --> 00:16:37,777
Pause the video now.

256
00:16:38,227 --> 00:16:43,048
The loops are one after another, not
nested. So it's n plus n, which is two n.

257
00:16:43,498 --> 00:16:46,485
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

258
00:16:47,635 --> 00:16:52,768
Let's count exactly how many times a
statement runs, like an AP multiple choice

259
00:16:52,768 --> 00:16:53,352
question.

260
00:16:54,502 --> 00:16:59,966
The problem: how many times does count
plus plus run? The outer loop runs i from

261
00:16:59,966 --> 00:17:03,654
zero to four. The inner loop runs j from i
up to four.

262
00:17:04,804 --> 00:17:09,732
Here's the code. The outer loop runs five
times, for i equals 0 through 4.

263
00:17:10,182 --> 00:17:14,428
The inner loop starts at j equals i, so it
gets shorter each time.

264
00:17:14,878 --> 00:17:17,224
count plus plus runs once per inner pass.

265
00:17:18,374 --> 00:17:23,217
Make a table. When i is 0, j runs from 0
to 4: five times.

266
00:17:23,667 --> 00:17:25,310
When i is 1: four times.

267
00:17:25,760 --> 00:17:27,381
When i is 2: three times.

268
00:17:27,831 --> 00:17:29,452
When i is 3: two times.

269
00:17:29,902 --> 00:17:31,225
When i is 4: once.

270
00:17:31,675 --> 00:17:35,643
Add them: 5 plus 4 plus 3 plus 2 plus 1 is
15.

271
00:17:36,793 --> 00:17:38,948
count plus plus runs 15 times.

272
00:17:39,398 --> 00:17:46,151
Shortcut: 1 plus 2 plus up to n is n times
n plus one, over two. For n equals 5,

273
00:17:46,151 --> 00:17:49,275
that's 5 times 6 over 2, which is 15.

274
00:17:49,725 --> 00:17:54,077
This shape is still about n squared over
two, so it grows like n squared.

275
00:17:55,227 --> 00:18:00,432
Your turn. Change both loops to use 6
instead of 5. How many times does count

276
00:18:00,432 --> 00:18:01,648
plus plus run now?

277
00:18:02,098 --> 00:18:03,293
Pause the video now.

278
00:18:03,743 --> 00:18:08,692
6 plus 5 plus 4 plus 3 plus 2 plus 1,
which is 21.

279
00:18:09,142 --> 00:18:13,366
Next, let's put the whole unit together in
one complete Java program.

280
00:18:14,516 --> 00:18:17,823
So far, we've learned each Unit 2 idea on
its own.

281
00:18:18,273 --> 00:18:23,621
Now let's use all of them together, in one
complete Java program that compiles and

282
00:18:23,621 --> 00:18:23,947
runs.

283
00:18:25,097 --> 00:18:29,471
Our program is called NumberReport. It
takes a number and prints a report:

284
00:18:29,921 --> 00:18:35,278
its factors, whether it's prime, perfect,
or composite, its value in binary, a true

285
00:18:35,278 --> 00:18:37,537
or false check, and a star pattern.

286
00:18:37,987 --> 00:18:40,483
Each part uses a different Unit 2 skill.

287
00:18:41,633 --> 00:18:47,347
Every Java program needs a class and a
main method. The class name must match the

288
00:18:47,347 --> 00:18:49,675
file name: NumberReport dot java.

289
00:18:50,125 --> 00:18:55,777
Inside main, we set n to 28 and print a
heading. Everything else goes inside main,

290
00:18:55,777 --> 00:18:56,397
in order.

291
00:18:57,547 --> 00:19:01,195
Part one: factors. A for loop tries every
f from one to n.

292
00:19:01,645 --> 00:19:06,829
If n mod f is zero, f is a factor. We add
it to the factors String, and count it.

293
00:19:07,279 --> 00:19:12,267
And if f is less than n, we also add it to
sum. That's an if inside a loop, with

294
00:19:12,267 --> 00:19:13,701
another if inside that.

295
00:19:14,851 --> 00:19:21,997
Let's trace it for 28. The factors are 1,
2, 4, 7, 14, and 28, so count is six.

296
00:19:22,447 --> 00:19:28,954
sum leaves out 28 itself: 1 plus 2 plus 4
plus 7 plus 14, which is 28.

297
00:19:30,104 --> 00:19:33,944
Part two: an if, else if, else chain.
Exactly one branch runs.

298
00:19:34,394 --> 00:19:39,478
If there are exactly two factors, the
number is prime. Otherwise, if sum equals

299
00:19:39,478 --> 00:19:42,245
n, it's perfect. Otherwise, it's
composite.

300
00:19:42,695 --> 00:19:49,145
For 28, count is six, so it's not prime.
But sum equals 28, so the program prints:

301
00:19:49,145 --> 00:19:50,247
28 is perfect.

302
00:19:51,397 --> 00:19:56,564
Part three: binary, with a while loop. m
mod 2 gives the last bit. We put it in

303
00:19:56,564 --> 00:19:59,311
front of the String, then divide m by two.

304
00:19:59,761 --> 00:20:06,379
For 28, the bits come out 0, 0, 1, 1, 1,
and the String builds up to one one one

305
00:20:06,379 --> 00:20:07,207
zero zero.

306
00:20:07,657 --> 00:20:09,662
The loop stops when m reaches zero.

307
00:20:10,812 --> 00:20:16,876
Part four: a compound boolean expression.
n must be at least 10, at most 99, and

308
00:20:16,876 --> 00:20:17,255
even.

309
00:20:17,705 --> 00:20:20,499
All three are true for 28, so it prints
true.

310
00:20:20,949 --> 00:20:26,027
With double ampersand, if one part is
false, Java skips the rest. That's short

311
00:20:26,027 --> 00:20:27,264
circuit evaluation.

312
00:20:28,414 --> 00:20:31,806
Part five: nested loops. The outer loop
runs four rows.

313
00:20:32,256 --> 00:20:36,971
The inner loop prints row stars, so we get
one, two, three, and then four stars.

314
00:20:38,121 --> 00:20:44,052
Now compile and run it. javac NumberReport
dot java compiles the code into a class

315
00:20:44,052 --> 00:20:44,414
file.

316
00:20:44,864 --> 00:20:48,213
java NumberReport runs it. Here's the
complete output.

317
00:20:49,363 --> 00:20:53,651
Your turn. Change n to 13. What are the
first four lines of output?

318
00:20:54,101 --> 00:20:55,296
Pause the video now.

319
00:20:55,746 --> 00:21:02,679
Report for 13. Factors: 1 and 13. 13 is
prime. Binary: 1101.

320
00:21:03,129 --> 00:21:06,223
Try it yourself. The full program is in
the description.

321
00:21:07,373 --> 00:21:11,731
If this helped, subscribe to School
Computer Science, Explained, so you don't

322
00:21:11,731 --> 00:21:12,919
miss the next lesson.

323
00:21:13,369 --> 00:21:14,201
See you there.

